# Action Audit and Observability Boundary

Action metadata can produce a structured audit envelope, but the host decides
when and where to persist it. This workbook separates denied attempts, failed
execution, and committed outcomes without a database.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_verification!()
```

## 1) Define a host-owned audit sink

The adapter records only after its simulated execution succeeds. `Agent` is an
ephemeral demonstration sink, not a production audit store.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/action_fixture.exs", __DIR__))

defmodule LivebookAuditedActionAdapter do
  def execute_action(plan, context, opts) do
    case Keyword.get(opts, :outcome, :ok) do
      :ok ->
        event = Map.put(context.action_audit, :outcome, :committed)
        Agent.update(Keyword.fetch!(opts, :audit_sink), &[event | &1])
        {:ok, %{action: plan.action, affected_rows: 1}}

      {:error, reason} ->
        {:error, reason}
    end
  end

  def dry_run_action(plan, context, _opts) do
    {:ok, %{action: plan.action, audit_preview: context.action_audit}}
  end
end

{:ok, audit_sink} = Agent.start_link(fn -> [] end)
domain = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.domain()
enabled = fn _request, _context, _opts -> :enabled end
denied = fn _request, _context, _opts ->
  %{status: :disabled, reason: "Approval limit exceeded", code: :approval_limit}
end

new_plan = fn ->
  {:ok, plan} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain,
    %{action: :approve, target: 1}, trusted_context: %{actor_id: 17})
  plan
end
```

## 2) Keep preview non-durable

The preview receives the same declared envelope with phase `:preview`, while the
committed-event sink remains empty.

```elixir
context = %{actor: %{id: 17}, tenant: %{tenant_id: 101}, preview_id: "preview-42"}
assert {:ok, preview} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.dry_run(
  LivebookAuditedActionAdapter, new_plan.(), context,
  capability_resolver: enabled, audit_sink: audit_sink
)
assert preview.audit_preview.event == "approval.approved"
assert preview.audit_preview.phase == :preview
assert preview.audit_preview.actor == %{id: 17}
assert Agent.get(audit_sink, & &1) == []
```

## 3) Distinguish denial and execution failure

A policy denial never invokes the adapter. An invoked adapter can still fail;
neither case is written as a committed outcome.

```elixir
assert {:error, {:validation_error, "Approval limit exceeded", %{code: :approval_limit}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
    LivebookAuditedActionAdapter, new_plan.(), context,
    capability_resolver: denied, audit_sink: audit_sink
  )

assert {:error, :simulated_conflict} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
  LivebookAuditedActionAdapter, new_plan.(), context,
  capability_resolver: enabled, audit_sink: audit_sink,
  outcome: {:error, :simulated_conflict}
)
assert Agent.get(audit_sink, & &1) == []
```

## 4) Persist the successful outcome

```elixir
assert {:ok, %{affected_rows: 1}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
  LivebookAuditedActionAdapter, new_plan.(), context,
  capability_resolver: enabled, audit_sink: audit_sink
)

assert [event] = Agent.get(audit_sink, &Enum.reverse/1)
assert event.event == "approval.approved"
assert event.phase == :execute
assert event.action == "approve"
assert event.capability == "approvals.approve"
assert event.target == 1
assert event.outcome == :committed
assert event.timestamp =~ ~r/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}T/

:ok = Agent.stop(audit_sink)
event
```

Production systems commonly record attempts and outcomes separately. Keep
secrets out of declared audit inputs/metadata, make sink failure policy explicit,
and correlate preview/apply using a host-generated identifier.
See [guarded execution](selecto_action_execution_workbook.livemd) for the native
PostgreSQL adapter path.
